lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135166874.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[Congress has raised concerns over the release of US national Matthew VanDyke, who is accused of terrorism. VanDyke was permitted to return home while protests against electoral irregularities continue. The National Investigation Agency is still investigating the Myanmar arms training case involving VanDyke and six Ukrainian nationals. The Congress leader criticized the government's approach to detaining Indian youth activists.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/no-hesitation-in-detaining-youth-congress-targets-government-over-us-national-vandykes-release-in-myanmar-arms-case/articleshow/135166874.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/no-hesitation-in-detaining-youth-congress-targets-government-over-us-national-vandykes-release-in-myanmar-arms-case/articleshow/135166874.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-24T15:43:28+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Yashasvi Vasistha</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135167814,imgsize-1596852.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Why do some women become the strongest defenders of patriarchy]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/why-do-some-women-become-the-strongest-defenders-of-patriarchy/articleshow/135165441.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135165441.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/why-do-some-women-become-the-strongest-defenders-of-patriarchy/articleshow/135165441.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/why-do-some-women-become-the-strongest-defenders-of-patriarchy/articlesh